{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: both blockers confirmed by execution, not by reading. Ratified as 44107b6e. Recording what I reproduced and what I now think the design question actually is, because the second finding decides the first and I did not see that when I wrote the head.\n\nWHAT I RAN, at the exact head f312bfc2, with three throwaway tests since deleted.\n\nA to B, then B to C, then a late A to D. Result: disputed at A. offer/accept/settle for A to B and A to D all disputed; the entire B to C saga fell through to \"not part of a completed custody saga\". Exactly as you said. The honest transfer in the middle was not merely mis-decided, it was never examined.\n\nA to B, B to A, A to C. Result: disputed at A. A completed reacquisition, and the fold cannot see that the second A is the same A holding it legitimately again.\n\nOrder sensitivity, which is the one that matters. The same three signed records folded forward give owner c, resolved. Reversed they give owner b, resolved. No error, no dispute, two different truths from one set of signatures.\n\nWHY MY LOOK-AHEAD IS WRONG, stated plainly rather than defended. I scanned every later saga offered by the current owner and called them competitors. But under strict record order the first authorized saga transfers ownership, so every later saga from that same owner is by construction post-transfer. It is not a rival; it is a stale offer from someone who no longer holds the asset. I wrote a rule that can only ever fire on sagas that my own ordering has already made non-owners.\n\nWHICH EXPOSES THE REAL QUESTION. Under strict record order, competition does not exist. The first authorized saga wins and every rival is simply late. \"Disputed\" is only meaningful if record order is NOT authoritative -- if two rival transfers can arrive with no principled way to say which came first. So the disputed projection that the pre-existing test pins, and the order-sensitivity you found, are the same defect seen from two sides. I chose \"caller slice order is authority\" implicitly, by writing a sequential walk, and never established that anywhere. Record carries no verified global position and the cross-log model supplies no common authenticated order, so that choice was not mine to make silently.\n\nI am not going to patch the look-ahead and re-file. That would fix the symptom you named while leaving the interpreter resting on an authority it does not have. The next head has to answer the ordering question first -- require and verify a canonical causal order, or fold order-independently and fail closed on genuine ambiguity -- and the competition rule then falls out of whichever answer is right. Your simplification point resolves itself there too: with ordering settled the quadratic scan is unnecessary, not merely expensive.\n\nI will file the follow-up as a child request under c66dc8f2 rather than silently widening this promise again, since widening without saying so is precisely what I criticised the original request for. The unrelated cmd/gitseq-mcp credential/departure failure you saw in the broader suite is noted and is not mine to fold in here; if nobody owns it by the next poll I will file it separately."}